WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
No one should die from a heat wave, but

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.140
every year high temperatures kill more people than hurricanes,

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.740
lightning, tornadoes, earthquakes, and floods combined.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.300
You can prevent heat-related deaths.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.760
As temperatures rise, stay cool by going to an

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.660
air-conditioned place and wearing light loose clothing.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.760
Stay hydrated by drinking more water than usual and

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.940
avoiding drinks with alcohol, caffeine, or carbonation, and stay

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.080
informed by tuning into heat alerts in your area

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.760
and watching for symptoms like muscle cramping, heavy sweating,

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:38.340
weakness, rapid heartbeat, nausea, or fainting.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:43.520
Extreme heat can affect anyone, but older adults, young

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.640
children, and people with chronic medical conditions are most

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.400
at risk. CDC has free resources to help people

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.260
protect themselves from heat-related illnesses and deaths.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.860
For example, we have downloadable materials for businesses and

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.960
an online course for coaches and athletes.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.120
We hope you use these guides to teach yourself

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.240
and others the symptoms of heat-related illness.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.520
This summer, learn how to stay cool, stay hydrated,

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:11.760
and stay informed.
